A||||6||gtsonly||IAC and TPS Adjusting||||5||
Z||000000||gtsonly||11-22-2000||01:37 PM||||Is there a way to test either of these sensors and if so, how? Can either of these be adjusted?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-22-2000||01:55 PM||||IAC is a Stepper Motor. It has no adjustment and other than a resistance test there's not much you can do to it. (Unless you have a stepper motor controller handy.)<P>The TPS is slightly adjustable on some motors. the little arm that runs it gets bent some times. (you can't adjust it at all on 87 & up 4 cyls.) These can be tested with an OHM meter and by measuring the TPS input voltage at the ECM. ECM scanners will also report this sensors position as a percent of WOT.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||11-22-2000||03:03 PM||||FWIW, a long time ago, I adjusted the TPS (by bending the little arm Ogre refers to) on my 1987 GT so its output voltage (measured at center terminal of TPS, with a high-Z digital voltmeter) was:<P>0.61 volts at closed throttle<BR>4.10 volts at wide open throttle (WOT)<P>...and I'm happy with the way it runs.  Didn't record the supply voltage, so don't know what the percentages (output votage/ supply voltage) are.  Anyway, might give you a starting point   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P>BTW, record the readings before you mess with it, so you can always go back to where you were, in case adjusting the arm actually makes things worse   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<BR>----------------------------------------<BR>Edited the above; replaced "battery voltage" with "supply voltage," per Ogre's comment.<BR> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||11-22-2000||04:45 PM||||The other than being way out, the ECM reads the TPS when you start the car and calls that 0%. 100% is then whatever the code is set to and it scales it in between. <P>Changing the position doesn't do a whole lot. It will move where power enrichment kicks in relative to the throttle plate, but I wouldn't expect it to be enough to be noticable.<P>Another good reason to no step on the gas when you start the car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-22-2000||06:15 PM||||sensor output has nothing to do with battery voltage. all the sensors, except the O2, run from the ECM's internal 5 volt supply. The O2 sensor generates it's own voltage. Never connect a sensor to battery voltage.||||reg||1
Z||000005||gtsonly||11-22-2000||06:22 PM||||how do you test the tps? I am to understand the center post is where you get your reading using a multi-meter but do you ground it anywhere there is a good ground or do you ground it to the plug itself to get the reading?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||11-23-2000||02:06 AM||||Chassis ground is fine or you can pick it up on the TPS.||||reg||1
